## INTRODUCTION
As a relatively new class of porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers, have attracted growing interests in recent two decades, due to their crystalline nature, tunable structures/properties and potential applications as functional materials in many fields.1-5 MOFs are constructed from metal ions/clusters and multi-topic organic struts linked through coordination bonds. Given the flexible and diversified coordination modes, the combination of the same organic ligand and metal unit may afford a variety of MOFs with distinct structures under similar or even identical reaction conditions.6 However, this also causes an undesired but common issue in MOF synthesis: two or more phases are formed in a one-pot reaction and the separation of them is difficult, which severely hampers the application of the obtained MOFs because even structurally similar MOFs have enormously different properties.6b,7 To address such an issue, Hupp, Fahar and coworkers have reported a solvent-assisted separation of mixed MOFs based on their density difference;8a metal-organic polyhdedra (MOPs) can also be separated similarly, which has been demonstrated by Yaghi and coworkers.8b Despite its effectiveness, the method is limited because the density difference has to be distinct and the separating parent solvent $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{BrCl}$ is toxic. Therefore, it is imperative to develop a general and eco-friendly approach to facilitate the separation of mixed MOFs that are prone to form in a one-pot reaction.
To obtain phase-pure MOFs, which can be assessed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), it is necessary to review the mechanism of crystal growth. In case of a homogeneous nucleation, clusters that are smaller than critical sizes are formed first in the system in a nearly supersaturated solution. Subsequently, different clusters may combine or split during the initial growth. These association and dissociation processes reach a dynamic equilibrium, possibly yielding a variety of MOF nuclei. Consequently, this leads to MOFs in mixed phases, because nuclei surpassing the critical size will evolve into crystals (Scheme 1, top). The energy required in the nucleation stage gradually increases to a maximum when nucleus of critical size attains and decreases thereafter (Scheme S1a).9
Scheme 1. Schematic illustration of the traditional process of MOF crystal growth (homogeneous nucleation) and the strategy for seed-mediated growth of phase-pure MOFs (heterogeneous nucleation).
In addition to the above homogeneous nucleation, heterogeneous nucleation of MOFs with matched crystal lattices also occurs when foreign particles/components are present (Scheme 1, bottom), which has been well established for large crystal growth by a seeded solution method.9,10 Moreover, the heterogeneous process requires much lower driving force than the homogeneous one (See Supporting Information, SI,  Section 1). In this sense, the introduction of seed crystals of the particular MOF into the reaction system would enable the growth of the particular MOF, which is kinetically favored, as the nucleation stage is bypassed. Meanwhile, once the MOF growth starts, the precursor concentration gradually decreases, making the nucleation and crystal growth of undesired phases more difficult, which results in the formation of pure target MOF product only (SI,  Section 2).
With the above considerations in mind, we explored seedmediated synthesis of phase-pure MOFs. Recently, Zrcarboxylate MOFs have been extensively studied due to their ultrahigh stability.11,12 Particularly, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs based on tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP) is highly soughtafter due to its built-in multifunctionality. We and others have reported diverse $Z\mathbf{r}$ -TCPP MOFs, exhibiting exceptional stability and various functionalities recently.12 These $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs are all obtained from $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ and TCPP ligand under similar reaction conditions. Consequently, the preparation of these MOFs in their phase-pure forms remains a formidable challenge. Moreover, the “accompanying” MOF pairs, which associate with each other throughout the synthetic procedure, are difficult to separate into phase-pure forms.12a-e Typical examples of these accompanying MOFs pairs are PCN-222(Ni) and PCN-224(Ni), PCN-222 and PCN-225, as well as PCN$222(\mathrm{Zn})$ and PCN-225(Zn) (the metal in the parentheses represents the metal ion residing in the TCPP center). These $Z\mathbf{r}$ - MOF pairs possess similar density and thus are hardly separated via the aforementioned solvent-induced method.8 Fortunately, all three pairs of MOFs (Figures 1a-c) can be prepared in phase-pure forms via a seed-mediated approach reliably, which would otherwise be difficult or impossible. In addition, a Cd-MOF triad, three MOF isomers with distinct pore structures (Figures 1d-f),13 usually obtained with impurities or accompanying one another, have also been prepared in their phase-pure forms via such a strategy, revealing its versatility. To the best of our knowledge, this is an initial report on the synthesis of phase-pure MOFs with a seed-assisted strategy.
## MATERIALS AND METHODS
Materials. Methyl 4-formylbenzoate, zirconium(IV) chloride, nickel(II) chloride hexahydrate, zinc(II) chloride, cadmi$\mathrm{um(II)}$ nitrate tetrahydrate, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy, and pyrrole were purchased from Energy Chemical. Propionic acid was from Aladdin Industrial Inc. 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{APTA)}$ was from Sigma-Aldrich. N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), N,Ndiethylformamide (DEF) was from Alfa Aesar. Acetic acid, and benzoic acid were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. The tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)-porphyrin $\mathrm{(H_{2}T C P P)}$ and [5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)- porphyrinato]- $\cdot\mathrm{{Ni}}(\mathrm{{II}})$ (Ni-TCPP) ligands were synthesized according to previous reports (SI, Section 3).12b All commercial chemicals were used without further purification.
Instrumentation. Powder X-ray diffraction patterns (PXRD) were collected in a Japan Rigaku SmartLabTM rotation anode X-ray diffractometer or a Holland X’Pert PRO fixed anode Xray diffractometer or a BRUKER D8-Focus Bragg-Brentano
X-ray powder diffractometer or a Rigaku MiniFlex600 X-ray Diffractometer equipped with graphite monochromatized $\mathrm{cu}$ $\mathsf{K a}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) was carried out with a field emission scanning electron microanalyzer (Zeiss Supra 40 scanning electron microscope at an acceleration voltage of $5~\mathrm{\kV})$ . Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted on a SHIMADZU DTG-60H thermogravimetric analyzer. ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms were measured using a Micrometritics ASAP 2420 or ASAP 2020 system.
Synthesis of MOFs via different recipes:
Recipe a: A mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(10~mg)}$ , Ni-TCPP $\mathrm{10~mg)}$ and benzoic acid $(350~\mathrm{mg})$ was ultrasonically dissolved in 1.75 mL DMF and $0.25~\mathrm{mL}$ DEF in a $4~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The mixture was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the products were harvested by filtration, washing and drying.
Recipe $\pmb{b}$ : A mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ $\mathrm{\Omega^{(40\mg)}}$ , benzoic acid $(2700~\mathrm{mg})$ and 20 drops of acetic acid was ultrasonically dissolved in $8~\mathrm{mL}$ DEF in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The mixture was heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the products were harvested by filtration, washing and drying.
Recipe c: A mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $\mathrm{70~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ $\mathrm{(50~mg)}$ , and benzoic acid $(2700~\mathrm{mg})$ ) was ultrasonically dissolved in 8 mL DEF in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The mixture was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the products were harvested by filtration, washing and drying.
Recipe d: A mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ ( $\mathrm{70~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ $\mathrm{(50~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\langle50~mg\rangle}$ , and benzoic acid $(2700~\mathrm{mg})$ was ultrasonically dissolved in $8~\mathrm{mL}$ DEF in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The mixture was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the products were harvested by filtration, washing and drying.
Recipe e: A mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta(60~mg)}$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ $\mathrm{(50~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{{ZnCl}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\nabla}50~\mathrm{mg})$ , and benzoic acid $(2500~\mathrm{mg})$ was ultrasonically dissolved in $9~\mathrm{mL}$ DEF in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The mixture was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the products were harvested by filtration, washing and drying.
Recipe $f\colon$ A mixture of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{108~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{APTA}$ $\mathrm{(47~mg)}$ , and BPY $\mathrm{41~mg)}$ was ultrasonically dissolved in 6.5 mL DMF in a $21~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined bomb. The mixture was heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the products were harvested by filtration, washing and drying.
Recipe g: A mixture of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{108~mg)}$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{APTA}$ $(47~\mathrm{mg})$ , and BPY $\mathrm{41~mg)}$ was ultrasonically dissolved in 4.4 mL DMF in a $21~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined bomb. The mixture was heated at $105~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooled naturally, the crystals were obtained. Due to the air sensitivity of microCd, the PXRD studies in air should be performed with a few mother liquid.
Recipe h: A mixture of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{108mg)}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{APTA}$ $\left(47~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ , and BPY $\left(41~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ was ultrasonically dissolved in $6~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF in a $21~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined bomb. The mixture was heated at $105~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . Due to the air sensitivity of mesoCd, the PXRD studies should be performed with a few mother liquid once it was taken out from Teflonlined reactor in air.
